/E (Used Af temperature as an indicator of expected radial force behavior, then measured Chronic Outward Force \(COF\) and Radial Resistive Force \(RRF\) on both old and new wire lots to confirm the relationship held across sources.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Old wire samples consistently showed slightly higher Af temperatures than new wire lots across all tuning times tested, with both sources following the same trend of increasing Af temperature as tuning time increased. Both wire sources landed within the acceptable Af range, with the old wire falling closer to the center of the target spec.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [30]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (The old wire's higher Af temperature corresponded to lower COF and RRF values relative to the new wire, consistent with a smaller delta between Af and body temperature driving lower force output. Both sources produced radial force curves with the same overall shape \(an initial peak followed by a gradual decline as diameter increased\), confirming that Af temperature remained a reliable predictor of radial force behavior across suppliers.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [31]
>>
endobj
